---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Stratigraphy Page Help">
    <section class="stratigraphy-section">
        <h2 class="text-teal-400 my-1.5">Stratigraphy Page Help</h2>
        <div>
            <p>
                Delving into the Stratigraphy page, this section is designed to provide you with detailed information about the stratigraphic layers found in
                the area you're exploring. The page is structured to give you insights into the types of rock layers, their geological context, and their
                significance in understanding Earth's history. You can expect to find information on various stratigraphic units, their age, and the
                environments they represent. The page may also include images and descriptions of notable stratigraphic features, as well as links to further
                resources for those interested in learning more about geology and stratigraphic research.
            </p>
            <p>
                <img
                    src="/src/assets/stratigraphic-dark.png"
                    alt="stratigraphy page dark mode"
                    style="float: right; margin: 0 0 1em 2em; width: 300px; max-width: 40vw; height: auto; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);"
                />
                This screenshot shows the stratigraphy page of the app in dark mode. The image shows the local stratigraphic records section, which provides information
                about the stratigraphic units found in the area you're exploring. It aims to be informative and visually engaging, with a focus on providing useful
                information about the stratigraphic units and their geological context. You can explore different stratigraphic units, learn about their age and
                significance, and view images and descriptions of notable stratigraphic features.
            </p>
            <p>
                Clicking on different age classifications will provide you with more detailed information about each stratigraphic unit, including its
                classification, age, and the environment it represents. You can also find links to further resources for those interested in learning more about
                geology and stratigraphic research. The stratigraphy page is a great way to explore the rich geological history of our planet and gain a deeper
                understanding of the ancient environments that once existed on Earth.
            </p>
            <p class="pt-15">
                <img
                    src="/src/assets/stratigraphic-explain-dark.png"
                    alt="stratigraphy detail page dark mode"
                    style="float: left; margin: 1em 2em 0em 0em; width: 300px; max-width: 40vw; height: auto; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);"
                />
                <p class="pt-8">
                    This screenshot shows the stratigraphy detail page of the app in dark mode. The image shows the more detailed information about a specific
                    stratigraphic units, including its classification, age, and the environment it represents. It aims to aid in the understanding of the
                    stratigraphic units and their geological context. You can explore different stratigraphic units, learn about their age and significance.
                </p>
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</Layout>
